WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.542
This slide asks you to strengthen a CHECK constraint.

00:00:04.542 --> 00:00:12.477
The clinic does not allow negative standard prices, but the current threshold is deliberately too weak.

00:00:12.477 --> 00:00:16.082
Run the example first so you understand its current behavior.

00:00:16.082 --> 00:00:22.072
Then change the threshold from negative one hundred to zero and run it again.

00:00:22.072 --> 00:00:28.382
After the correction, the negative test value should be rejected.

00:00:28.382 --> 00:00:30.262
In this exercise, rejection is success.

00:00:30.262 --> 00:00:34.797
It means PostgreSQL is enforcing the business rule.

00:00:34.797 --> 00:00:39.987
Pay attention to the difference between fixing the data and fixing the rule.

00:00:39.987 --> 00:00:45.907
We are changing the schema constraint so future invalid values are also protected,

00:00:45.907 --> 00:00:49.182
not only correcting one row.

00:00:49.182 --> 00:00:53.342
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:53.342 --> 00:01:01.062
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:01:01.062 --> 00:01:05.652
and the shape of the expected output.

00:01:05.652 --> 00:01:11.232
In a real setting such as a university system separating students, courses, sections, and enrollments,

00:01:11.232 --> 00:01:19.167
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:01:19.167 --> 00:01:20.282
before discussing syntax.

00:01:20.282 --> 00:01:28.522
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
